---
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import LayerStripe from '../components/LayerStripe.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { project } from '../lib/project';

const updated = '14 November 2026';
---

<Base
  title={`Privacy policy · ${project.name}`}
  description={`How ${project.name}, its website, playground, npm package and Claude Code plugin handle your data.`}
>
  <LayerStripe />
  <Header />
  <main class="container policy">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p class="lead">
      {project.name} doesn’t collect, store or sell personal data. There are no accounts, no analytics, no tracking
      cookies and no advertising in any part of the project.
    </p>

    <h2>This website (archimaid.org)</h2>
    <p>
      The site is static. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics and loads no third-party scripts or fonts. It is hosted
      on Cloudflare, which processes technical request data, such as IP addresses, to deliver and protect the site. See
      the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The playground (try.archimaid.org)</h2>
    <p>
      Diagrams are parsed and rendered in your browser. Your diagram and your theme, notation and export preferences are saved in your
      browser’s local storage, so they never leave your device. Clear them by clearing the site data for
      try.archimaid.org.
    </p>
    <p>
      A share link holds your diagram in the part of the URL after <code>#</code>. Browsers don’t send that part to the
      server, so the diagram reaches only the people you send the link to. The playground is also hosted on Cloudflare.
    </p>

    <h2>The npm package and command-line tool</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>{project.packageName}</code> package and the <code>archimaid</code> command run on your own machine.
      Validating and rendering diagrams makes no network requests and sends no telemetry. Installing the package
      downloads it from the npm registry, which is covered by the{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/privacy">npm privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The Claude Code plugin</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/Archimaid/claude-plugin">{project.name} plugin</a> contains instructions and
      commands only. It has no hooks, no MCP servers and no telemetry, and it doesn’t read credentials. When Claude
      validates a diagram, it runs <code>npx archimaid validate</code> on your machine with your approval, which
      downloads the package from npm as described above. What you share with Claude is covered by{' '}
      <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy">Anthropic’s privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      Issues, discussions and pull requests on <a href={project.repository}>GitHub</a> are public and covered by the{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub privacy
      statement</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      Changes to this policy are published on this page and in the{' '}
      <a href={`${project.repository}/commits/main/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}>repository history</a>. For questions,{' '}
      <a href={project.newIssue}>open an issue</a>, or report a concern privately through the repository’s{' '}
      <a href={`${project.repository}/security`}>Security tab</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 760px;
    padding-block: 96px 40px;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 5vw, 56px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 48px 0 12px;
    font-size: 22px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .updated {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .lead {
    margin-top: 32px;
    font-size: 20px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
